This study examines how Discipline-Based Education Research (DBER) postdoctoral associates build professional agency for instructional change through repeated cycles of self-agentification and recognition within critical tensions. Drawing on interview data from postdoc-faculty dyads across three STEM disciplines at a single R1 institution, we identify five archetypical patterns of agency development that show how resources and liabilities accumulate differently depending on postdoc strategies and institutional conditions. A key finding is that no single archetype applies to an individual across all contexts — developmental patterns shift depending on the relational and structural environment. Agency is not granted by the position itself; it accumulates (or fails to) through a postdoc's active moves in response to tensions, and only when those moves are met with recognition from established change agents.